# Suppressions for reachable memory held by the glibc dynamic linker (ld.so)
# when getaddrinfo(3) lazily loads an NSS service module (libnss_dns.so.2,
# libnss_mdns4_minimal.so.2, ...) via dlopen(3).
#
# These blocks are owned by ld.so's internal bookkeeping (link_map structs,
# library path strings, symbol-version hash tables) and are intentionally
# kept live until process exit. glibc loads NSS modules through its own
# private loader (__nss_module_load -> __libc_dlopen_mode) and never calls
# a matching dlclose-equivalent; the mapping cannot be released from
# application code, even by dlopen()/dlclose()-ing the same module yourself
# first (that only manipulates your own independent reference, not glibc's
# internal one). They are not leaks.
#
# Two suppressions cover all variants because the allocations go through
# either malloc (for link_map structs and strdup'd path strings) or calloc
# (for _dl_new_object and _dl_check_map_versions tables), with _dl_open
# always present somewhere in the call chain above them.

{
   ld_so_dlopen_reachable_malloc
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   fun:malloc
   ...
   fun:_dl_open
}

{
   ld_so_dlopen_reachable_calloc
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   fun:calloc
   ...
   fun:_dl_open
}
